    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • XPENG Reveals G9L at the 2026 Paris Motor Show, Dubbed As the Ultimate \"Large SUV for the AI Era\"\n2025 Cadillac XT6 Hits the Market for Under Half the Price at 11,400 Miles. Steal or Deal?\n27 photos\nPhoto: eBay\nThis 2025 Cadillac XT6 was listed for sale for half the sticker, even though it has logged under 11,400 miles in less than a year since rolling off the production line. However, it’s not all good news. The vehicle was crashed and repaired, and is now for sale with a Rebuilt title.\n\"Loaded with features.\" This is how the seller describes this 2025 Cadillac XT6 Platinum Premium Luxury, right before a long list of equipment. The crossover came with Super Cruise and Night Vision, front bucket seats, front and rear heated seats, a heated steering wheel, automatic multi-zone climate control, keyless entry and start, navigation, hands-free power liftgate, and a cabin wrapped in leather and suede, and the list goes on and on.\nInside that cabin, airbags were deployed just a few months ago. Photos in a previous listing, where it was sold for $14,500, show the steering wheel cut open by the airbag deployment and the knee airbag hanging in the footwell.\nThe front passenger's airbag also inflated, while the rear side curtain airbags snapped to protect the heads of the occupants. And that doesn’t tell half the story. The Cadillac XT6 was involved in a collision that severely deformed the front end.\nPhoto: America Auto The front lower trim came apart, the grille was shattered, the bumper was cracked and contorted, while the hood was crumpled backwards. The driver's door was also damaged. Furthermore, a photo of the engine bay shows that it obviously needed internal work as well.\nThe engine block seems intact, but the large radiator, the front fascia, and headlight brackets (possibly headlights as well), along with the cooling fans and hood latch assembly, must have been replaced due to either cracking or misalignment caused by the accident that pushed them inward.\nLuckily, the car did run and drive even after the crash, which means that the 3.6-liter V6 engine, code-named LGX, escaped the front of the impact. The V6 produces 310 horsepower (314 metric horsepower) and 271 pound-feet (367 Newton meters) of torque.\nThat is what makes the vehicle accelerate from 0 to 60 mph (0 to 97 kph) in 6.9 seconds on its way to a top speed of 132 mph (212 kph). A nine-speed GM Hydra-Matic automatic transmission puts the power down through the front wheels. The gearbox also escaped intact.\nPhoto: America Auto The accident took place just months after the XT6 had been delivered, at 11,316 miles (18,211 kilometers), as indicated by the photos taken right after the crash, which makes it still a new car. But it is a new car with a Salvage title that was rebuilt to factory specification, which makes it look like it just rolled off the production line.\nCadillac discontinued the XT6 , which leaves the used car market as the only source for the model. A dealer is selling the repaired Cadillac, now having 11,414 miles (18,369 kilometers) on the clock, for $31,995. When new, the 2025 Cadillac XT6 Platinum Premium Luxury with Front Wheel Drive started at $66,290, which means this vehicle is for sale for under half the original sticker price. The seller is offering it with a Rebuilt Title that can be registered in all 50 states.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\n2025 Cadillac XT6 Platinum Luxury Cadillac Cadillac XT6 crashed Cadillac Cadillac XT6 price\nAbout the author: Elena Luchian\nElena started off as a sports reporter and five years later switched to automotive. How does that happen? Lots of writing, testdrives, traveling, off-road driving courses, track driving courses, defensive driving courses and diplomas. The diplomas might be \"useless\", but the knowledge she gained is priceless. Full profile\nUP NEXT Forgotten Legends: 5 Defunct American Carmakers and Their Greatest Production Models",
